 "final_text": "`/work/putnam_2012_b4.lean` now compiles with no errors and no `sorry` warning. The answer is yes: `a n - log n` tends to 0.\n\nThe proof uses `b n = exp (a n)`:\n\n1. `b (n+1) = b n * exp (1 / b n)`, and `exp x \u2265 x + 1` gives `b (n+1) - b n \u2265 1`. So `b n \u2265 n + 1`, and `b` tends to infinity.\n2. The derivative of `exp` at 0 gives `b (n+1) - b n \u2192 1`.\n3. Mathlib's Ces\u00e0ro lemma (`Tendsto.cesaro`) then gives `b n / n \u2192 1`, since the differences sum to `b n - b 0`.\n4. Taking logs, `log (b n / n) = a n - log n \u2192 log 1 = 0`.\n\nI only replaced the `sorry`. The imports, theorem statement and `putnam_2012_b4_solution` are unchanged.",
